{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","title":"Doppler effect with circular wavefronts"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Each emitted pulse expands as a circle. A moving source leaves each circle centered where that pulse was emitted, not where the source is now.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Doppler effect: moving source"},{"op":"figure","id":"waves","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-6.7,3.3],"y":[-4.6,4.6],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"oldcenter","at":[-2,0]},{"kind":"circle","id":"oldwave","center":{"at":"oldcenter"},"r":4},{"kind":"point","id":"source","at":[0,0],"label":"source now"}],"meaning":"An expanding circular wavefront retains its original emission center."}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","step":{"id":"s2","script":"Let the source move right at half the wave speed, emitting equally often. Newer circles are smaller and have centers farther right.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"newwaves","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"point","id":"center2","at":[-1.5,0]},{"kind":"circle","id":"wave2","center":{"at":"center2"},"r":3},{"kind":"point","id":"center3","at":[-1,0]},{"kind":"circle","id":"wave3","center":{"at":"center3"},"r":2},{"kind":"point","id":"center4","at":[-0.5,0]},{"kind":"circle","id":"wave4","center":{"at":"center4"},"r":1},{"kind":"segment","from":[-2,-0.35],"to":[0,-0.35],"arrow":true,"label":"motion"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"speed","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"v_s=\\frac{v}{2},\\qquad \\lambda_0=\\frac{v}{f_s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s3"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","step":{"id":"s3","script":"During one emission period, the source advances. Wavefronts bunch together ahead and spread apart behind; their spacing is the wavelength.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"spacing","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"measure","from":[1.5,0],"to":[2,0],"label":"λ ahead"},{"kind":"measure","from":[-6,0],"to":[-4.5,0],"label":"λ behind"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"wavelengths","anchor":{"el":"speed","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"\\lambda_{\\mathrm{ahead}}=\\frac{v-v_s}{f_s}=\\frac{\\lambda_0}{2},\\qquad \\lambda_{\\mathrm{behind}}=\\frac{v+v_s}{f_s}=\\frac{3\\lambda_0}{2}"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"For a stationary listener ahead of the source, is the observed frequency higher or lower?","expected_gist":"Higher: closer wavefronts arrive more frequently."}}}
{"type":"lesson.warning","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","code":"step_repaired","step_hint":"s4"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","step":{"id":"s4","script":"Stationary listeners receive wavefronts at wave speed. Shorter spacing means higher frequency ahead; larger spacing means lower frequency behind. The source's emission frequency stays unchanged.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"frequency","anchor":{"el":"wavelengths","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"f_{\\mathrm{obs}}=\\frac{v}{\\lambda},\\qquad f_{\\mathrm{ahead}}=2f_s,\\qquad f_{\\mathrm{behind}}=\\frac{2}{3}f_s"},{"op":"figure","id":"listeners","on":"waves","marks":[{"kind":"point","at":[2.7,0]},{"kind":"text","at":[2,1.15],"text":"higher f","size":"small"},{"kind":"point","at":[-6.4,0]},{"kind":"text","at":[-5.4,1.8],"text":"lower f","size":"small"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.done","request_id":"f165545a-e7be-4dc3-af89-054322016a11","accepted_steps":4,"repairs":2,"dropped_steps":0,"sanitized_steps":0,"sanitized_fields":0}
